# Environment of k3s, and through containerd of the smolvm-pods shim.
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin:/opt/k3s/bin:/opt/k3s/bin/aux
# smolvm keeps VM data under SMOLVM_DATA_DIR, a disk volume. floci-node copies
# the agent rootfs and libkrun there at boot and puts the disk templates in
# $SMOLVM_DATA_DIR/.smolvm, so that VM data and templates share one mount.
SMOLVM_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/smolvm
SMOLVM_AGENT_ROOTFS=/var/lib/smolvm/runtime/agent-rootfs
SMOLVM_LIB_DIR=/var/lib/smolvm/runtime/lib
# smolvm run as root drops each pod VM's VMM to a uid of its own, which cannot take on the
# uid of a guest process: a non-root process in a pod (Istio's sidecar runs as 1337, as do
# most apps) then fails with EPERM on every write to a volume or /dev/shm, which the
# virtiofs share serves through the VMM. "off" keeps the VMM root, as it is on a Mac. The
# node VM, not the VMM's uid, is what bounds a pod here.
SMOLVM_VM_UID_DROP=off
# smolvm's hot reload watches each VM's shares on the host and has the guest look up every
# path that changes. The pod share changes only while a container's image root is copied
# into it, and cp gives a directory its mode and owner only after its contents: the guest
# then caches a directory such as /app as root's, mode 0700, and the container's overlay
# keeps that copy, so a non-root process gets EACCES under it for good. Nothing in a pod
# needs a share's changes, since volumes are copies too. Any value turns hot reload off.
SMOL_NO_HOT_RELOAD=1
RUST_LOG=info
# The memory, in GiB, and the CPUs that kubelet reports for the node. They
# bound the sum of all pods' requests, awake and asleep, so they are set far
# above the node's own. Only the wake admission caps below protect the node's
# real memory. 0, or a value below the node's own, reports the node's own. See
# node/README.md, "Capacity". floci-node reads them each time it starts k3s.
FLOCI_NODE_MEMORY_GIB=256
FLOCI_NODE_CPUS=128
# The node service's API over TCP, for Floci, as ADDR:PORT; empty for none.
# Every request needs the bearer token in /etc/floci-node/api-token, which
# floci-node creates at boot if it is missing. See node/README.md, "The node
# service's API over TCP". floci-node reads it each time it starts the node
# service.
FLOCI_NODE_API_LISTEN=0.0.0.0:7070
# When an idle VM pod sleeps: after FLOCI_NODE_IDLE_TIMEOUT with no traffic on
# connections to it, unless its VM used more than FLOCI_NODE_CPU_THRESHOLD
# vCPUs on average over FLOCI_NODE_CPU_WINDOW. FLOCI_NODE_CPU_THRESHOLD=off
# (or 0) lets busy pods sleep too. Durations are Go durations such as 30s or
# 5m. A pod's annotations smolvm-pods.floci.io/idle-timeout, cpu-threshold and
# cpu-window override them. See node/README.md, "Idle sleep". floci-node reads
# them each time it starts the node service; empty leaves the node service's
# own default.
FLOCI_NODE_IDLE_TIMEOUT=5m
FLOCI_NODE_CPU_THRESHOLD=0.5
FLOCI_NODE_CPU_WINDOW=60s
# Wake admission: a wake is admitted only while fewer than
# FLOCI_NODE_MAX_AWAKE_PODS pods are creating, awake, pausing or waking, and
# while their guest RAM with the waking pod's, 1024 MiB for each pod VM, stays
# within FLOCI_NODE_MAX_AWAKE_MEMORY MiB. A wake that does not fit waits up to
# FLOCI_NODE_ADMISSION_WAIT, then is refused. An empty or 0 cap is no cap, and
# with no cap nothing keeps awake pods within the node's real memory. See
# node/README.md, "Wake admission". floci-node reads them each time it starts
# the node service; empty leaves the node service's own default.
FLOCI_NODE_MAX_AWAKE_PODS=
FLOCI_NODE_MAX_AWAKE_MEMORY=
FLOCI_NODE_ADMISSION_WAIT=
